<h3>1:05:54 — Next session: mix static + instance (this video stops here)</h3>
<p>Take very special care. Here we executed child class; internally, creating a child object does the sequence they now know.</p>
<p>Next: mix instance control flow and static control flow, generate one or two examples, so they get much more clarity. He starts that thought (“Anyway…”) and the video ends at 1:06:38 — the mixed examples are not in this lecture (they belong to the next video). Do not invent extra mixed programs here.</p>
<h2>Board rules from this video (keep these)</h2>
<p>Whenever a Java class is executed, static control flow always starts first.</p>
<p>Static control flow:</p>
<ol><li>Identification of static members (top → bottom).</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execution of static variable assignments and static blocks (top → bottom).</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execution of main.</li></ol>
<p>If no object is created, instance members are not even identified. Output of the first Test without new: only main.</p>
<p>Whenever an object is created (anywhere), instance control flow runs:</p>
<ol><li>Identification of instance members (top → bottom). Original values; instance variables go RIWO.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execution of instance variable assignments and instance blocks (top → bottom). After assignment: R&amp;W. Calling m1() from an instance block can print a later instance variable as 0.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execution of constructor.</li></ol>
<p>Static CF = one-time, at class loading. Instance CF = every `new`.</p>
<p>Object creation is the most costly operation — do not create objects without a requirement.</p>
<p>Child object (parent-to-child) — five instance steps after static CF:</p>
<ol><li>Identify instance members parent → child.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Assignments + instance blocks parent only.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Parent constructor.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Assignments + instance blocks child.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Child constructor.</li></ol>
<p>Then remaining child main. java Child does not run Parent.main.</p>
